WEBVTT

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:09.070
Like other commuters in the South Bay, the people who work at Ames Research Center

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.530
have to navigate the same earthbound traffic as everyone else.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:18.010
But between the hangar that once housed a 1930s airship and the largest wind tunnel

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:21.820
is a world that takes them to the boundaries of human exploration.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:50.100
In the 1960s, the concept of a space shuttle was pure science fiction

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:56.370
until NASA and President Nixon announced the space transportation system in early 1972.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:00:59.980
This space vehicle would...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.990
launch like a rocket to low-Earth orbit. Its crew would release satellites and deliver parts for

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:13.510
the space station. A fiery plunge back into the atmosphere would turn the vehicle into a glider

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:20.950
that could be piloted to a controlled landing on a runway. It would be the most complex machine

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:29.970
ever built. April 12, 1981, NASA successfully launched STS-1, the first shuttle mission.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:38.460
Safely accelerating this machine to 17,500 miles an hour required years of sophisticated aerodynamic tests.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.700
Over half of all wind tunnel tests for the shuttle were done at Ames.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.990
These facilities provided an unmatched range of sizes and speeds.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:58.680
In the 1970s, Ames tunnels allowed scientists to actually see complex shock waves created by the shuttle before the first orbiter, Columbia, ever flew.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.730
The same wind tunnels conducted safety tests of new fuel tank features in 2005 before the shuttle returned to flight status.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:14.630
Ames computer simulations began in the 1970s and then improved exponentially.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.380
Today they can simulate speeds and conditions no wind tunnel could match.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.680
Work done on the Columbia supercomputer helped put the shuttle back into flight in 2005.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.250
The shuttle has performed many transportation tasks while in orbit.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.520
Dozens of satellites and spacecraft were launched from the payload bay.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.120
One was Galileo, developed in part by Ames.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.990
It eventually spent years orbiting Jupiter, collecting new data and images.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.000
More recently, shuttles have carried components of the International Space Station.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:10.150
All astronauts agree the perspective from orbit is breathtaking, but not all of them are comfortable in zero gravity.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:18.370
Now that humans are spending more time in this environment, the study of how it affects living organisms is critical for the future of spaceflight.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.840
AIM's Life Sciences Division explored this subject for over 40 years.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:28.080
Some experiments were done on the ground, and others were conducted on the shuttle.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:29.970
Flight experiments were installed.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.660
Installed in specifically designed lab modules carried in the shuttle's payload bay.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:42.260
Starting in April of 1991, Ames participated in shuttle missions such as Space Lab Life Sciences 1 and 2,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:48.120
International Microgravity Labs 1 and 2, Space Lab J, and NeuroLab.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:53.130
These studied the effects of spaceflight conditions on plants, animals, and humans.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.780
Now we have a much better understanding of how zero-gravity affects our balance and visual perception.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:06.550
how muscles, bone, and blood are affected, how immune systems change, and how plants grow.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.760
Results have already helped astronauts adapt to different gravity levels on missions

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.090
and recover faster after landing.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.950
Next to launch, re-entry is the most dangerous part of a shuttle's flight.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.970
Ames developed a technique in 1953 that has been used by all American scientists.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.300
spacecraft since then. AIM scientist Harvey Allen rendered 1950s space plane designs obsolete.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:42.960
He showed that a blunt body, not a pointed one, could survive reentry better by creating

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.420
a shock wave that would act as another heat shield. This idea was used to create the

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:54.540
shapes for Mercury, Gemini, Apollo and every other American space vehicle. Although the

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.370
shuttle uses the same principle, it still needs insulation from extreme heat. Starting

00:04:59.370 --> 00:04:59.970
in the 19th.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.740
AIMS scientists developed feather-like insulation tiles.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.880
AIMS also developed the largest arc jet wind tunnel. This high-speed

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.840
blow torch allowed them to test tile material at 3,500

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.680
degrees, the same temperature as the outer surface of a shuttle during

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:22.180
re-entry. These reusable tiles were so successful, they were incorporated

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:27.190
into most of the shuttle's main heat shield surfaces. To a shuttle lands,

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:29.330
the stress on the tiles is obvious.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.860
to help the technicians check thousands of tiles for damage,

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.500
Ames developed a handheld laser scanner

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.580
that speeds the process considerably.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:43.370
After debris damaged the leading edge of Columbia's wing in 2003,

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:47.150
the Arcjet was tasked with testing proposed patch materials.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.760
The resulting test data was fed into the Columbia supercomputer,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.880
which further predicted reliability on re-entry.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.840
shuttle during descent has been described like a brick with wings. An early question was how to

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:11.510
give it effective flight control. AIM's earlier innovations provided a foundation for the

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:17.740
shuttle's basic concept. Starting in 1957, Al Eggers and Cy Cybertson developed and tested

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:23.440
lifting bodies, simple shapes that could survive re-entry and provide just enough lift and control

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:29.980
so they could glide unpowered to a safe landing. High altitude drop tech

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.420
at Edwards Air Force Base in the 1960s proved their airworthiness.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:40.540
Before the Orbiter Enterprise was flight tested in 1977,

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:44.840
Ames did extensive wind tunnel tests of the shuttle perched atop

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:49.720
its 747 carrier aircraft. Starting in 1970,

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:54.180
long before a final shuttle design, a flight simulator at Ames

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.260
allowed astronauts to fly approaches while testing prototype flight controls.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:06:59.970
Astronauts Gordon Fuller

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.900
and Fred Hayes practiced in a larger and movable simulator at Ames before climbing into the Enterprise.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:13.660
Minor control problems on the first flight were ironed out in the same simulator to improve later tests in 1977.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.920
Commuters who come the farthest to Ames are the shuttle pilots and commanders.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:29.980
Starting with the crew of the first mission, STS-1, every shuttle pilot and commander has practiced hundreds of landings

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.980
in AIMS Vertical Motion Simulator.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.700
It offers the most realistic motion available.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.030
The head-up display developed here

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:39.500
was incorporated into Columbia's cockpit

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.560
before the first flight in 1981

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.260
and is a part of every orbiter today.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.570
AIMS wind tunnel tests also prove the safety

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:51.890
of the long-distance carrier

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:53.590
that returned the shuttle to Florida

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:55.150
after landings in California.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.960
Today, NASA is developing the multi-person

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.260
purpose crew vehicle. This Apollo-shaped capsule will be used for exploration missions beyond

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:11.340
low Earth orbit. Ames will contribute the highest level of scientific expertise and

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:14.160
innovation to create this new space vehicle.

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:24.560
And like they have for over 50 years, the AIM staff is still working with one foot in Silicon Valley and the other at the edge of human exploration.
